+# 017: The server assigns every name the user did not type
+
+Status: accepted
+
+Date: 2026-10-11
+
+Source: [M3 proposal](../../internal/core/M3-core-namespace-proposal.md), decisions D1 and D8.
+
+## Context
+
+The architecture specification split naming. On `path_taken` the client renamed
+to `Name (2).md` and retried; conflict copies were created by the client after
+`rebase_required` or `file_deleted`, and by the server after a stale `put_blob`.
+A client cannot choose free names reliably. Policy-excluded and not-yet-pulled
+files are invisible to it, and it cannot compute the path key, which is defined
+in Go only (ADR 018). Client-side naming would also require a TypeScript case
+folding that matches Go's exactly, and a retry loop whose bound depends on luck.
+
+## Decision
+
+The server names every file whose final path the user did not type.
+
+- `create` and `rename` carry `on_taken`: `number` (the default) or `reject`.
+  With `number`, the server runs `rules.FreeName` inside the transaction that
+  holds the `path_key` index and applies the operation under the result.
+- Conflict copies are requested with `create {copy_of: {file_id, reason}}` and
+  always carry the conflict marker, even when the original path is free, so an
+  edit to a deleted file never reads as an undelete.
+- The change and the `ack` carry the final `path` and `class`; the client
+  renames its local file to match.
+- A PDF renamed with its sidecar is numbered as a pair, so the pairing survives.
+- Ownership of the `Canvas/` subtree is a rules function: class C under the
+  Canvas root is bridge-only, compared by path key; classes A and B are
+  user-owned everywhere.
+
+Rejected: client-chosen names with retry (unreliable, needs a matching fold in
+TypeScript); server rejection without suggestion (pushes the same problem to
+the client).
+
+## Consequences
+
+One naming function exists, in Go, tested once. Names are deterministic given
+the occupied set. M4 gains `on_taken` and `copy_of`, and the ack carries the
+final path. A device briefly holds a new file at its requested path until the
+ack arrives. `path_taken` remains for `on_taken = reject` and for exhausted
+attempts (`MaxAttempts`, default 100). Invariant 2 is unaffected: copies only
+add files.
