+# 019: Classify complete content, server-authoritative, with no in-place class change
+
+Status: accepted
+
+Date: 2026-10-11
+
+Source: [M3 proposal](../../internal/core/M3-core-namespace-proposal.md), decisions D4 and D5.
+
+## Context
+
+A file's class is fixed at creation. The Implementation Guide classified from
+the first 8 KiB and deferred the full check, which lets a file with a NUL at
+byte 9,000 become class A permanently. The specification was silent on files
+whose content stops fitting their class after creation: a note growing past
+8 MiB, an external editor saving a note in a legacy encoding, a sidecar caught
+half-written, or a Freedraw upgrade the pinned schema no longer parses. The
+client sends a class with `create`, and the server must not trust client input.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Classification uses the complete content. `Expect` decides from name and size
+  (opaque results are final, so large opaque files are never read for this);
+  `Confirm` checks the full bytes. Class A and B ceilings (8 and 16 MiB) bound
+  the cost.
+- A failed sidecar demotes to class C, never to class A, consistent with ADR 011.
+- The server re-runs `Confirm` on the bytes it commits, including initial text
+  that arrives through the blob path, and rejects a disagreement with
+  `class_mismatch {class, demotion}`. The client resubmits with that class.
+- Classes never change in place and never upgrade. When a local change no
+  longer fits its class, the client waits for a quiet period (default 10 s,
+  set by measurement), then creates its local bytes as a server-named conflict
+  copy of the new class and restores the server head at the original path.
+  A server `too_large` on a class A edit takes the same path.
+
+Rejected: 8 KiB head classification (permanent misclassification); server
+silently storing a different class (the engine would have to write blobs to
+Garage inside the commit path); in-place `reclassify` (another atomic
+multi-row operation for a rare event).
+
+## Consequences
+
+Nothing is lost when content drifts out of its class: the bytes go to a copy and
+the head is intact (invariant 2). The user sees the original path revert, with a
+notice. A demoted file stays class C even if its content becomes valid again.
+`obsync_class_mismatch_total` should stay at zero; a nonzero value is a bug or
+version skew between plugin and server.
