+# 021: Reconciliation window per file class
+
+Status: accepted
+
+Date: 2026-10-11
+
+Supersedes: [009](009-transform-window.md)
+
+Source: [M3 proposal](../../internal/core/M3-core-namespace-proposal.md), decision D7.
+
+## Context
+
+ADR 009 bounds text merges by 24 hours and 5,000 operations, because very old
+overlapping text edits converge into unreadable interleavings. It was written
+for class A only. Class B element operations are not transformed, so their age
+and count do not degrade the merge; the only unsafe case is a base older than
+the tombstone prune cutoff, where a put could resurrect an erased stroke (M2).
+Class C has no merge at all, only a base-version check. ADR 009 also did not say
+whose clock measures the 24 hours.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Class A: rebase is required when the ops after the base are pruned, when more
+  than 5,000 ops separate base and head, or when the base is more than 24 hours
+  old. Exactly 5,000 ops and exactly 24 hours are inside.
+- Class B: rebase is required only when the base predates the tombstone prune
+  cutoff. The cutoff is the same value M10 uses to prune.
+- Class C: no window; a stale put becomes a conflict copy (ADR 017 names it).
+- Age is the server's current time minus the server's commit time of the base
+  version. Client-reported offline durations never enter the decision
+  (invariant 5).
+
+The thresholds remain configurable starting values, to be revisited after the
+S4 soak with `obsync_transform_depth` data.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Sidecar edits from a device offline for days still merge by element, which is
+what makes Freedraw annotation offline-friendly. Text behaviour is unchanged
+from ADR 009 apart from the explicit clock and boundaries. `rules.Reconcile`
+holds all three cases in one table-tested function.
